---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { REPRO, shortDigest } from '../lib/reproducibility'

const generatedLabel = new Date(REPRO.generated_at).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
  year: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  day: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
})
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Reproducibility — Tebako"
  description="The per-leg reproducibility record of the tebako Ruby runtime: which platform legs carry a byte-identity proof, the twin-runs that prove them, and the dispatch that re-proves any published leg."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 pb-20 pt-32">
    <p class="tb-eyebrow tb-animate-rise">玉手箱 REPRODUCIBILITY</p>
    <h1 class="tb-animate-rise tb-delay-1 mt-3">The reproducibility record.</h1>
    <p class="tb-animate-rise tb-delay-2 mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
      A tebako runtime arrives as two prebuilt artifacts — the interpreter executable and its
      environment image — and running one means trusting bytes that were compiled on someone else's
      machine. The Ruby runtime factory answers with a claim that can be checked: for a release tag
      whose tree carries the reproducible-build pins, two runs of the same tree produce a
      byte-identical executable, compared before any signing, and a byte-identical environment image.
    </p>
    <p class="tb-animate-rise tb-delay-3 mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
      The claim is owned by the factory's{' '}
      <a href={REPRO.source.doc} class="text-[var(--tb-c-accent)] hover:underline">docs/reproducible.md</a>,
      and the factory's{' '}
      <a href={REPRO.source.workflow} class="text-[var(--tb-c-accent)] hover:underline">reproduce.yml</a>{' '}
      workflow is its public check: one dispatch rebuilds one published leg on the release tag's own
      pipeline and compares the rebuilt bytes against the published record. This page renders the
      claim per leg of the{' '}
      <a href={REPRO.source.release.url} class="text-[var(--tb-c-accent)] hover:underline">{REPRO.source.release.tag}</a>{' '}
      release line from a checked-in data file whose every cited run is re-verified live on each
      regeneration.
    </p>

    <div class="tb-animate-rise tb-delay-3 tb-card mt-10 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-8 gap-y-4 p-6">
      <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        {REPRO.counts.proven} proven · {REPRO.counts.pending} pending · {REPRO.legs.length} legs
      </p>
      <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        release line {REPRO.source.release.tag} · ruby {REPRO.source.release.ruby_version}
      </p>
      <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        data generated {generatedLabel}
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="legs-heading">
      <p class="tb-eyebrow">PER LEG</p>
      <h2 id="legs-heading" class="mt-2 font-[var(--font-display)] text-3xl font-medium">
        The claim, leg by leg.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        One row stands per platform leg of the release line. A proven row names the twin-run that
        proves it — two builds of the same tree on independent runners, compared byte for byte — and
        a pending row says pending and points at the dispatch that proves a leg; no row says
        anything in between.
      </p>
      <div class="tb-prose mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Leg</th>
              <th>Status</th>
              <th>The claim</th>
              <th>The proof</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {REPRO.legs.map((leg) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  <p class="font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">{leg.label}</p>
                  <p class="mt-1 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
                    {leg.platform} × {leg.arch}
                  </p>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <span
                    class:list={[
                      'rounded-full px-2.5 py-0.5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs',
                      leg.status === 'proven' && 'border border-[var(--tb-c-divider-strong)] text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]',
                      leg.status === 'pending' && 'border border-dashed border-[var(--tb-c-divider-strong)] text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]',
                    ]}
                  >
                    {leg.status}
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{leg.claim}</p>
                  <p class="mt-1 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
                    exe scope: {leg.exe_scope}
                  </p>
                  {leg.digests.map((d) => (
                    <p class="mt-1 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
                      {d.label}{' '}
                      <span title={d.complete ? d.value : undefined}>{shortDigest(d)}</span>
                    </p>
                  ))}
                  {leg.note && (
                    <p class="mt-2 max-w-md text-xs leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">{leg.note}</p>
                  )}
                </td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  {leg.evidence ? (
                    <>
                      <p class="text-sm text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">{leg.evidence.name} twin-run</p>
                      {leg.evidence.runs.map((r) => (
                        <p class="mt-1 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs">
                          <a href={r.url} class="text-[var(--tb-c-accent)] hover:underline">
                            run {r.id}
                          </a>{' '}
                          <span class="text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]" title={r.sha}>· {r.sha.slice(0, 7)}</span>
                        </p>
                      ))}
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <a
                      href={`#verify-${leg.id}`}
                      class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--tb-c-accent)] hover:underline"
                    >
                      the proving dispatch ↓
                    </a>
                  )}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="scope-heading">
      <p class="tb-eyebrow">SCOPE</p>
      <h2 id="scope-heading" class="mt-2 font-[var(--font-display)] text-3xl font-medium">
        What the claim covers, and what it does not.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <p>
          Signing rewrites the executable after the claim's digest is taken: macOS applies codesign
          today, and Windows applies Authenticode once that signing work arms. A signed artifact is
          therefore irreproducible per run by design, and the executable claim on the signing
          platforms is scoped to the pre-signing digest that both builds record in their
          provenance. The published, signed executable verifies against its own sha256 sidecar,
          never against a rebuild.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each build records a contract card, which is the build's provenance record: the source
          archives' digests, the pre-signing executable digest, and the toolchain that ran,
          including the card's builder <span class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">os:</span>{' '}
          field naming the machine that built it. Two proving runs whose{' '}
          <span class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">os:</span> fields differ are not a leak in
          the claim; the field is the provenance record doing its work, and the compared digests
          are taken from the bytes, not from the machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three parts of the toolchain are recorded rather than pinned. On macOS the brew formulae
          float within a runner image: the Brewfile pins formula names, and the resolved versions
          land in the toolchain record. On Windows, MSYS2 tracks a rolling repository and no
          snapshot mirror exists to pin against, so byte-identity on the Windows legs is claimed
          while the recorded pacman resolution stands, and the resolution's digest is part of the
          provenance record. The CI runner image floats on the provider's schedule and is recorded
          in the toolchain record as the suspect to read first when a comparison fails.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pins landed after v0.17.15, and a tag whose tree predates them stamps its image
          manifests with wall-clock time, so its images can never byte-reproduce. For those tags
          reproduce.yml verifies the published-integrity floor — the published executable and image
          download and sha256-verify against their published sidecars — and the identity rows of
          the verdict light up automatically on the first release whose tree carries the pins.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-20" id="verify" aria-labelledby="verify-heading">
      <p class="tb-eyebrow">VERIFY</p>
      <h2 id="verify-heading" class="mt-2 font-[var(--font-display)] text-3xl font-medium">
        Re-prove any published leg.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        <p>
          One dispatch of reproduce.yml verifies one published leg. The workflow checks out the
          release tag's own tree, dispatches the platform's build on the tag ref with publishing
          disabled, and renders a verdict whose hard rows compare the rebuilt bytes against the
          published record: the published executable and image against their sha256 sidecars, and
          on releases that carry the pins the rebuilt executable and image against the published
          digests. The workflow holds no write grant, so a verification run can never create,
          replace, or delete an asset of the release.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="tb-prose mt-6 max-w-3xl">
        <pre><code>gh workflow run reproduce.yml --repo tamatebako/tebako-runtime-ruby \
  -f tag=&lt;vX.Y.Z&gt; -f ruby_version=&lt;e.g. {REPRO.source.release.ruby_version}&gt; \
  -f platform=&lt;linux-gnu|linux-musl|macos|windows&gt; -f arch=&lt;x86_64|arm64&gt;</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-2)]">
        Each leg of the current release line carries its dispatch below. A red verdict names the
        artifact and both hashes and quotes the published provenance record's toolchain, and the
        run's machine-readable verdict is downloadable as the run's{' '}
        <span class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm">reproduce-verdict-*</span> artifact.
      </p>
      <div class="tb-prose mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Leg</th>
              <th>The dispatch</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {REPRO.legs.map((leg) => (
              <tr id={`verify-${leg.id}`}>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap">
                  <p class="font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">{leg.label}</p>
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs break-all">{leg.verify.command}</code>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 border-t border-[var(--tb-c-divider)] pt-8">
      <h2 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg font-medium text-[var(--tb-c-text-1)]">
        How this page is built
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 max-w-3xl space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--tb-c-text-3)]">
        <p>
          The claim and its scope are authored in the Ruby runtime factory's docs/reproducible.md,
          and the evidence of record is the factory's CI. This page renders a checked-in data
          file, and the site build never fetches.
        </p>
        <p>
          A generator script produces the data file and holds it to the factory's record on every
          run: the recorded legs and their exe scopes must match the factory's own per-leg table
          cell for cell, reproduce.yml must still carry the documented dispatch inputs, and every
          cited run must be completed, green, and on the recorded commit in the GitHub API. The
          proven unsigned Linux leg is additionally anchored against the published release's
          sha256 sidecar, so the page cannot drift from the bytes the release actually serves. A
          failed check fails the regeneration and leaves the committed file untouched.
        </p>
        <p>
          The data file regenerates daily and on dispatch from the factory, and the drift lands as
          a data-only pull request. A reproduce.yml verdict does not write to this page today;
          wiring the workflow's verdict feed into the data file is the designed next step, so that
          a fresh verdict updates the record without a human edit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
